export default {"id":"docs-managed-key-service","path":"docs/managed-key-service.md","title":"Managed key service","parts":[{"id":"part-1","title":"Managed key service","html":"<h1 id=\"managed-key-service\">Managed key service</h1>\n<p>The launch profile specifies <strong>a hosted software key service and authenticated\nage-private-key retrieval</strong> on Cloudflare or Netlify. It does not require paid AWS/GCP/Azure KMS,\nnew paid identity, upgrades or external services at launch. Authorized profile\nenrollment provisions both crypto and age associations; this does not release\nan offline key, activate hybrid or grant arbitrary decrypt/billing authority.\nKMS is configured per profile; the launch custody claim is explicitly <strong>software</strong>.</p>\n<p>The software custody profile uses a versioned wrapping key held in protected host secrets <strong>outside the database</strong>, with\nindependently random per-profile DEKs. Vetted maintained crypto performs the\nactual wrap/unwrap operations. A secrets store alone is not a key service.\nThe runtime can access/use its software key; shared KEK or authorized-runtime\ncompromise can expose all reachable profile DEKs. Random DEKs do not make that\nindependent non-exportable/HSM custody. This supersedes the earlier requirement\nfor an external provider-native KMS at launch, without changing core auth,\ntenant isolation, secret handling, durable required audit or engineering gates.\nProfiles requiring stronger hardware/non-exportability remain unavailable here.</p>\n<p>Future managed/HSM custody is retained as an explicit funded migration path,\nnot silently claimed for the launch. Mode/first-run choices remain explicit;\na remote-first mode is not inferred from configuration or provider availability. Synthetic/mock functional user, account, operator and administrator UI previews\nmay be developed separately, including loopback dashboard local-configuration\nedits and simulations. They remain non-production: no real backend, operational\nkey, provider or account access, deployment, purchase or account connection is\nauthorized.</p>\n<p>First run initializes the hosted key service by default, after configuration\nselection and authentication, unless specifically disabled on the command line\nor in configuration. Resolve either opt-out before any hosted-service network\ncall; apply documented precedence and enterprise locks without silently\nregistering through a conflict. Registration and bounded future key-release\nwindows are enabled for the managed profile. Self-hosted, offline and\nhosted-disabled profiles remain supported. <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-management-and-discovery&amp;part=part-1&amp;anchor=first-run-and-minimal-managed-registration\">First-run discovery and registration</a>\nresolves policy and authenticates before registration; it uploads neither\npersonal configuration nor secret values by default. The selected policy and\nservice authorize mappings and future releases, not arbitrary secrets from the\nclient. Registration alone cannot grant a key.</p>\n<p>Expanded custom admin UI/screens remain unapproved for production operation.\nThe bounded synthetic/mock preview authorization does not change that limit; use\nexisting platform services and the confirmed first-run CLI/text authentication\nflow for actual operations.\nThe base service needs the user/client/domain association, owner-authorized key\nmapping/storage/retrieval, access-window decisions, safe operational records and\nmetering. Enterprise hierarchy, SCIM, GitHub/GitGuardian gates, provider rotation,\nworkload federation, posture and OPA are optional CircularQuay capabilities,\nnot assumed base-subscription components or costs. Exact-bundle remote\nauthorization and a wider management control plane remain separate explicitly\nchosen profiles; genuine hosted KMS itself is now required.\nSecurity, tenant isolation and secret-free diagnostics still apply to the small\nservice; simplicity does not make authentication or key authorization optional.</p>\n<p>The service controls <strong>future private-key release</strong> during its authorized\nwindow. Age gives a private key no intrinsic expiry. A retained private key can\nstill decrypt matching old ciphertext after registration, subscription or\nretrieval access ends; copied keys/ciphertext cannot be remotely revoked.\nApplication credential rotation, recipient changes and remote unlocking are\nseparate mechanisms with their own residual limits.</p>\n<p>This is the intended product contract, not a deployed endpoint or qualified\nrelease. <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-implementation-plan&amp;part=part-2&amp;anchor=managed-service-development-and-delivery-contract\">Delivery requirements</a>\nand <a href=\"https://github.com/maldous/CircularQuay/blob/feature%2Finitial-implementation/docs/qualification.md\" title=\"Repository reference (may differ from this local snapshot)\">revision-bound qualification</a> establish actual evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"authorized-software-launch-custody-and-provider-choice\">Authorized software launch custody and provider choice</h2>\n<p><strong>Preferred low-cost candidate:</strong> existing Workers Paid + D1 + native Workers\nsecret bindings/Web Crypto, with R2 encrypted recovery/archive only within the\nexisting authorized Cloudflare footprint. Use a separate custody Worker/service\nidentity and narrowly bound encrypted key database; account/support runtime must\nnot receive the KEK or unrestricted crypto binding. Keep the KEK outside DB,\nrepository, build/client/preview output and logs. Generate independent random\nprofile DEKs, use maintained crypto and the approved immutable crypto/envelope\nprofile, unique nonces and authoritative tenant/profile/purpose/key-version AAD.\nThe standard format/adapter and scoped authenticated API still need implementation;\nno bespoke cipher, public-ID derivation or unverified SOPS compatibility.</p>\n<p>The documented Workers Paid floor is $5/month before overages/archive. This is\n<strong>conditionally feasible</strong>, not proof of the user&#x27;s existing tier or remaining\ncapacity. It also needs an actually qualified existing/free customer/workload\nissuer; Cloudflare account login/Access alone is not customer identity. Do not\nadd a paid IdP to make the forecast work. When that identity route is absent,\nexisting eligible Netlify Personal ($9 base) or Pro with included Identity and\nproduction-scoped Functions/secrets/database is the conditional alternative.\nIdentity protocol/sender/MFA fit, secret/role/audit controls and current entitlements\nmust be verified. The gateway has supplied <strong>no verified current paid-tier/project/\nissuer metadata</strong>, so no actual deployment can honestly be selected as qualified.\nSelect the cheapest feasible branch after owner-held readback; do not connect\naccounts, purchase a plan/pack or deploy both hosts by implication.</p>\n<p>Software launch changes the custody guarantee, not the meaning of authorization.\nExact current tenant/profile/operation/key version/purpose/windows, possession/\nreplay controls, deny-before-upstream access, bounded retries, durable required\naudit and final pre-effect checks remain mandatory. Use separate control-plane,\ncrypto and customer-support identities with least privilege; separate stores or\nbindings count only when access is independently enforced. No plaintext keys in\nadmin diagnostics, telemetry or generic MCP responses. Rotation/recovery keys\nremain protected under explicit custodians; an old backup cannot revive denied\npolicy or an older key/version. Software storage secrecy does not protect against\nan authorized compromised crypto runtime or privileged deployment writer.</p>\n<p>Both crypto and age associations remain automatic only inside an authenticated,\nauthorized idempotent enrollment with durable partial-state reconciliation.\nExisting local keys/assets need the separate migration workflow below. Online-only\nhas no offline recipient bypass. Explicit hybrid activation needs compatible\nciphertext/snapshot, recipient/key versions, window/freshness/audit/fences and\navailability-only fallback; denial/TLS-trust/malformed/expiry cannot downgrade.\nRetained plaintext/data keys/age keys remain usable and cannot be recalled.</p>\n<p>For funded stronger custody, retain logical key IDs, purpose/algorithm/envelope\nversions, provenance, grants/windows and protected recovery references. Qualify\nan actual managed SOFTWARE/HSM backend, rewrap only where the format and old/new\nauthority permit, or create a fresh data key and re-encrypt locally as required.\nStage and verify new artifacts, restore/denial generations and permitted hybrid\npaths before atomic activation; keep originals until separately approved\nretirement. Future provider cost/permission is not preapproved. Neither software\nlaunch nor later migration can claim an earlier copied key never existed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"per-profile-age-and-kms-custody\">Per-profile age and KMS custody</h2>\n<p><strong>KMS</strong> is configured in the <strong>user profile</strong>. Treat custody choice and workload availability mode as\nseparate validated decisions. These names describe contracts, not invented\nruntime enum values. Both associations are provisioned by the required hosted enrollment behavior;\nselection of active custody/mode remains explicit. This capability is not yet\nimplemented by the inspected CircularQuay SOPS adapter.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Profile custody choice</th>\n<th>Required configuration and authority</th>\n<th>Boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Local age</td>\n<td>Exact approved recipient and protected local identity reference; local filesystem/device permissions and recovery policy.</td>\n<td>No KMS or hosted key retrieval is implied. Retained age private keys decrypt matching ciphertext offline.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hosted age-private-key retrieval</td>\n<td>Exact trusted service, resolved identity, tenant/profile mapping and release window; separately authorized registration/key access.</td>\n<td>Original minimal hosted service returns a copyable age private key. It is not remote KMS master-key custody.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future/explicit external KMS</td>\n<td>Selected provider, account/project/tenant, region, exact key resource/version policy, permitted encrypt/wrap/decrypt/unwrap operations, declared auth identity, approved endpoints and recovery policy.</td>\n<td>Master-key custody belongs to the selected native KMS behind the service, or an explicitly selected third-party provider. Reuse established backends/SDKs; only the authorized provisioning flow may create resources.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Provider references must be unambiguous: AWS key ARN/account/region and exact\nassumed-role/workload identity; GCP project/location/key-ring/key and key-version\npolicy plus service identity; or Azure tenant/vault/key/version policy plus\nmanaged/workload identity, according to the selected supported adapter. Validate\nresource ownership, region/residency, purpose, key state/algorithm and minimum\noperation grants; never infer KMS permission from a secret-store reader&#x27;s role.\nKeep bootstrap credentials protected client-side and prefer qualified short-lived\nworkload identity; no ambient human session or broad provider fallback. A\nreference to a provider key is not a key-creation or account-connection grant.</p>\n<p>The inspected <code>internal/source/sops.go</code> explicitly rejects SOPS <code>kms</code>, <code>gcp_kms</code>,\n<code>azure_kv</code>, <code>huawei_kms</code> and <code>key_groups</code> metadata. It accepts constrained age-only\nor Transit-only paths; snapshot creation supplies age recipients only. Its\nisolated subprocess environment is not a cloud-KMS identity implementation.\nUpstream <a href=\"https://getsops.io/docs/\">SOPS supports cloud KMS backends</a>, but this\ndoes not establish support in CircularQuay. Existing cloud secret-reader SDKs\nlikewise do not establish a KMS adapter. Require actual schema, source, help,\nidentity and executable qualification before offering a provider choice.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Workload availability mode</th>\n<th>Required KMS/fallback behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Online-only (<code>online</code> in the existing workload model)</td>\n<td>Required KMS must succeed; no local age, hosted-age or snapshot bypass. Unsupported KMS configuration fails explicitly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explicit hybrid (<code>hybrid</code>)</td>\n<td>Preserve the authorized downgrade: a qualifying availability failure may restore a complete approved encrypted offline snapshot within its policy window. Require explicit offline permission, matching scope/policy, valid freshness and no denial fence; audit-required profiles cannot use the current fallback path.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offline (<code>offline</code>)</td>\n<td>Use the explicitly approved local ciphertext/identity path. Do not claim that offline use is currently authorized by a remote KMS on every decrypt.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Current resolver eligibility requires hybrid, a configured snapshot, allowed\nfallback, no required provider audit and <code>Unavailable</code>; restore performs further\nscope/freshness/fence checks. Denied access, TLS/trust failure, malformed response,\nexpired data and revoked authority are <strong>not qualifying availability failures</strong>.\nDo not silently change mode, classify denial as outage, or add an undeclared age\nrecipient. Test both permitted hybrid downgrade and prohibited downgrade. This\npreserves established hybrid behavior; it does not assert the missing cloud-KMS\nadapter has been built or qualified.</p>\n<p>Ordinary multiple SOPS master-key recipients are <strong>alternative unlock paths</strong>:\none available key can recover the data key. Upstream\n<a href=\"https://getsops.io/docs/usage/identities/key-groups/\">key groups</a> support a\nconfigured threshold across group fragments, but this adapter currently rejects\ngroups. Do not promise AND/quorum without implementing and verifying the actual\nmechanism, threshold and every recovery path. An age fallback that can recover\nthe data key permits offline decryption. KMS custody keeps its master key remote,\nbut the client can retain unwrapped data keys/plaintext; a hostile client cannot\nbe forced to contact KMS for every future decrypt. Policy windows cannot remotely\nexpire retained keys or erase plaintext.</p>\n<p>The same resolved profile, validation, mode/fallback rules, authorization and\nsafe errors must apply to CLI, API, SDK and MCP. Unknown/unsupported provider\nfields must fail, never be ignored. Config/explain shows safe provider/key\nreferences and effective mode, never credentials or key bytes. Intended client\nUI exposes the same complete choices and limits locally. Synthetic/mock\npreviews and loopback local-configuration simulations are authorized separately;\nproduction or real-backend UI implementation remains unapproved. No made-up\ncommand, endpoint or MCP method follows from this contract.</p>\n<p>Authorized first-run enrollment now idempotently provisions <strong>both</strong> KMS and\nage associations under the resolved user/client/tenant profile. Honor the\nestablished hosted CLI/config opt-out and enterprise locks before network;\nprovision neither through a conflict. Provisioning can create/reuse actual\nprovider key resources only in an authorized implemented enrollment flow, under\nexplicit service budgets/quotas and compensation rules. It grants no offline-key\nrelease or workload access by itself. No account is connected or paid key created\nin this documentation assignment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"future-managed-kms-implementation-boundary\">Future managed KMS implementation boundary</h3>\n<p>For a future separately selected/funded managed profile, implement an authenticated\nwrapping/unwrapping path backed by a provider-native KMS, separately from the\nage-private-key endpoint. This is not a launch dependency. An actual\ncandidate is AWS KMS symmetric <code>Encrypt</code>/<code>Decrypt</code> for protected data keys, or\n<code>GenerateDataKey</code> when that established envelope format is appropriate. AWS\n<a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/APIReference/API_Encrypt.html\">Encrypt</a>\naccepts bounded small payloads; it is not a general configuration upload API.\n<a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/APIReference/API_Decrypt.html\">Decrypt</a>\nreturns the permitted plaintext data key; pin the expected key resource rather\nthan trusting a client-selected ciphertext/key identifier. Provider master-key\ncustody and key policy are real KMS controls; Workers secrets, Netlify environment\nvariables and database EAR do not themselves provide this remote KMS capability.\nAWS is a researched candidate, <strong>not a selected provider or default</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bind each call to the authenticated tenant/profile, operation, logical key ID,\npurpose, exact provider key/version and applicable window. Server-side association\nlookup supplies the permitted key; reject arbitrary key IDs and cross-profile\nciphertext/context. Use the provider&#x27;s established ciphertext format and SDK,\nnot a custom cipher. AWS symmetric\n<a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/encrypt_context.html\">encryption context</a>\nis authenticated and also logged in plaintext, so use opaque safe identifiers,\nnever configuration, credentials or sensitive customer fields. Separate runtime\ncrypto grants from resource provisioning, deletion and account administration;\nrestrict provider identity, endpoints, rate/bulk operations, logs and recovery.</p>\n<p>All user config/variables/source credentials stay client-side. A KMS call may\nprocess an authorized protected data key; it does not ingest the user&#x27;s bundle\nor become remote config orchestration. Upstream SOPS AWS/GCP/Azure integrations\nare not automatically compatible with a custom hosted HTTP endpoint. Qualify a\nreal supported SOPS/key-service adapter and protected transport, exact key\nreferences and envelope metadata; until implemented, report unsupported. The\ncurrent adapter rejects cloud-KMS and key-group metadata, so neither automatic\ndual provisioning nor hosted KMS operation support is yet established by source.</p>\n<p>Provision dual associations through a durable, tenant-bound idempotency record:\ntrack KMS and age resource state separately, reconcile retries/concurrency and\npartial failure, and do not report ready until both required associations exist.\nDo not orphan duplicate paid keys or retry-charge customers after uncertain\noutcomes. Record logical IDs, provider resources, public age recipients,\nversions/status, grant/window references and safe provisioning state only.\nWhere independent protection/revocation/windows are required, use separate key\nIDs and independently enforced custody scopes; a shared master key plus labels\nis not cryptographic isolation. Key-sharing strategy and availability mode are\nexplicit design decisions, not defaults supplied by this document.</p>\n<p>An online-only artifact must have no authorized offline recipient path.\nProvisioning its age association alone does not add such a path. An explicit\nauthorized hybrid transition must produce or verify a compatible artifact version\nwith the intended KMS-wrapped data key and approved age fallback recipient,\nusing the supported SOPS mechanism. Reconcile recipient/key versions, approved\nwindow, scope and snapshot freshness atomically before activation. Ordinary\nrecipients are alternatives; do not invent AND semantics. Do not simply assume\nAWS KMS symmetric ciphertext can be decrypted with age: the fallback needs its\nown valid wrapping of the same intended data key or an explicitly generated\ncompatible snapshot. Subsequent online-only activation may require a fresh data\nkey/artifact without offline recipients; copied prior keys/data remain usable.</p>\n<p>The authorized software launch deliberately uses native host secrets plus vetted\ncrypto, not an external native KMS. User-facing crypto operation authority and\ninternal database KEK custody remain separate purposes. A future managed/HSM\nprofile is separately funded, selected and qualified, with no launch dependency. This does\nnot require deploying both Cloudflare and Netlify or buying a second KMS for\nunrelated internal storage. Actual provider, key scopes, costs, auth and recovery\nremain explicit qualification decisions.</p>\n"},{"id":"part-2","title":"End-to-end local-to-managed migration","html":"<h2 id=\"end-to-end-local-to-managed-migration\">End-to-end local-to-managed migration</h2>\n<p>Migration is a required product workflow, <strong>not a mode flag</strong>. No local key\ninventory, operational key read/upload, asset conversion or live provider setup\nhas been performed or authorized here. Start with a client-local, read-only\nmetadata inventory within explicitly selected paths/providers; never scan\nunrelated keychains or secret stores. Keep full configuration, variables and\nsource credentials on the client throughout.</p>\n<p>Inventory protected source references and ownership, public age recipient IDs,\nOS/hardware/self-hosted/provider key definitions and export policy, ciphertext\nformat/tool/version and digest, recipient/key versions, profile/tenant/domain,\ngrants/windows, approved availability mode, recovery paths and dependent assets.\nDo not hash private keys as identifiers or write decrypted values into manifests.\nReconcile duplicates/aliases, missing keys, unknown provenance and conflicting\nassociations before any migration effect. Produce an exact plan of source,\ndestination, unchanged originals, proposed new versions, permissions, recovery,\ncost and unsupported cases. A metadata inventory is not permission to export.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Existing material</th>\n<th>Safe candidate path and constraints</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Exportable local age identity</td>\n<td>Reuse its recipient locally, or explicitly import its protected private identity into the authenticated age service with separate owner consent and verified encrypted transport/storage. An imported key has historical copies; managed custody cannot become retroactively non-exportable. Alternatively create a new managed recipient and migrate assets.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OS-protected identity/secret</td>\n<td>Verify actual provider API, user/service context and export rights. Protected-file/DPAPI storage is not a portable KMS key export; <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/security/how-to-use-data-protection\">DPAPI binds protection to user/computer context</a>. Where authorized original-host decryption is possible, re-protect through the supported target; never copy an opaque protected blob and claim migration succeeded.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-exportable hardware or provider master key</td>\n<td>Retain its supported remote reference as an explicitly selected external provider, or use an authorized unwrap/re-encrypt operation to create a compatible destination envelope. Do not attempt to extract the master key, relabel a copied public key as private custody or claim arbitrary age keys import into hardware. If no supported recovery/unwrap path exists, report the asset unmigratable until its owner supplies a legitimate route.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exportable self-hosted KMS material</td>\n<td>Check source export capability/permission, target algorithm/key-size/import format and loss/recovery semantics before considering BYOK. <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/importing-keys.html\">AWS import</a> accepts specified key types, not an arbitrary age identity or self-hosted ciphertext format. Matching raw key material does not make provider ciphertext formats interchangeable. Import is a separate explicitly approved ceremony.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Existing encrypted asset</td>\n<td>Rewrap the existing data key only if the supported format can update recipients without changing payload semantics and the old authority can authorize unwrap. Otherwise decrypt/re-encrypt locally with vetted tools into a new protected artifact. Use a new data key when removing exposed/retained-key access requires protection for future artifact versions; a recipient metadata edit alone cannot revoke an already learned data key.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kms/docs/key-import\">Google KMS import</a> similarly\nrequires supported binary symmetric or DER PKCS #8 asymmetric formats, an\nauthorized wrapped import job and a permitted protection level. These examples\ndo not choose a destination provider. Exact OS/self-hosted export policy,\nhardware restrictions and provider-specific ciphertext compatibility remain\nper-adapter acceptance requirements, not assumed migration capabilities.</p>\n<p>Upstream <a href=\"https://getsops.io/docs/usage/key-management/\">SOPS key management</a>\ndistinguishes updating master-key recipients from rotating the data key and\nre-encrypting values. Reuse those actual mechanisms with a pinned supported\nadapter; the current CircularQuay adapter still rejects cloud-KMS/key-group\nmetadata. Neither a hosted KMS endpoint nor a target key reference proves\nSOPS/plugin/OS ciphertext compatibility. Maintain per-format migration support\nand independent native/provider integration evidence; unsupported cases deny.</p>\n<p>Execute only an approved bounded plan under separate source-read/export,\ndestination-provision/import/wrap, profile-change, offline-export and recovery\ngrants. Recheck tenant/profile/purpose/version, current permission and approval\nafter waits and before each effect. The authorized product flow provisions both\nKMS and age associations, but neither imports existing local keys nor releases\nan offline key by implication. Independent protection/windows need distinct\nkey IDs/custody authority where required by the selected security profile.</p>\n<p>Use a durable migration ID bound to source/destination/artifact digests and\nexpected revisions: planned → prepared → transformed → destination-verified →\nactivation-ready → activated → separately-approved retirement. Each asset has\nits own state and reconciliation receipt. Preserve originals and provenance;\nstage output in protected client-local storage and verify authenticated decrypt,\nexact contents, intended recipient/version/mode and recovery before atomic\nactivation. Do not write plaintext intermediates to general temp files, logs or\nmodel context. Cancellation, partial provider failure and uncertain responses\nreconcile existing destination resources, never create duplicate paid keys or\nannounce a partially migrated estate as complete. Keep plaintext lifetimes short.</p>\n<p>Online-only destinations have no offline recipient bypass. Hybrid activation\nmust be explicitly authorized and validate compatible KMS/age wrapping or an\napproved snapshot, grants, window, freshness, audit and denial fences. Availability\nfailure alone can trigger eligible hybrid fallback; denial, invalid TLS/trust,\nmalformed response, old/revoked version and expiry cannot. Test restart/resume,\nconcurrent revisions, revoked permission mid-flight and required-audit failure.\nRecovery preserves accepted generations and known denials; an old backup is not\npermission to roll policy back. Keep required audit durable before protected\neffects, with secret-free opaque references rather than client config uploads.</p>\n<p>Rollback restores only an authorized protected prior generation with its still\nvalid grants and fences; it cannot erase a completed import or copied plaintext.\nRetiring/deleting original keys, artifacts or provider resources is a separate\napproved action after dependency and restore checks. Historical ciphertext,\nexported keys and retained data keys/plaintext remain usable by their holders.\nRe-encryption protects new versions, not previously copied data. Record that\nresidual risk and actual issuer-secret rotation needs without claiming recall.</p>\n<h3 id=\"existing-high-security-assurance-applies\">Existing high-security assurance applies</h3>\n<p>KMS, local adapters and migration inherit <a href=\"/explore#doc=security&amp;part=part-1\">SECURITY.md</a>, the\n<a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-threat-model&amp;part=part-1\">threat model</a>, <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-administration-security&amp;part=part-1\">administration controls</a>,\n<a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-security-profiles&amp;part=part-1\">default/strict security profiles</a> and the\n<a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-assurance&amp;part=part-1\">assurance contract</a>. The explicit launch software-custody tradeoff changes the custody claim; it\ndoes not lower engineering, auth/isolation/audit or first-party gates. Stronger\nrequired custody profiles remain unavailable.\nUse maintained backends/vetted crypto with immutable crypto profiles, separated\ncustody and operation identities, scoped authenticated requests, replay/fallback\ncontrols, rotation/recovery and verified non-exportability claims. Required\nunknown capabilities deny; a flag or library label is not evidence.</p>\n<p>For each existing applicable control ID record the enforcement point, exact\nsource/artifact/provider identity, positive and negative outcomes, platform scope\nand result. Exercise denial before upstream access, cross-tenant/profile/purpose,\nwrong/old/revoked versions, replay, cancellation/uncertainty, interrupted migration,\nrequired-audit loss, allowed outage fallback and forbidden downgrade. Real backend\nintegration, recovery and qualified hardware evidence remain distinct from mocks.\nMissing authorized backend is <strong>unqualified</strong>, never a pass. The existing zero\nfirst-party issues/complexity/coverage/mutation and independent-review release\nrequirements remain unchanged. No certification or production-security pass is\nclaimed by this design or its static documentation checks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"client-configuration-is-outside-hosted-storage\">Client configuration is outside hosted storage</h2>\n<p>All configuration is read, resolved, fully validated and saved on the browser/\nnative client through the user&#x27;s own local files, URLs, network/VPN and source\ncredentials. It never enters this hosted backend, a server-side proxy,\nanalytics or logs; this includes corporate config as well as personal config,\ninclude contents and source credentials. Client-only explain/provenance does\nnot upload them as metadata. Browser CORS/file permissions remain real; use\nexplicit native CLI/SDK/local integration when needed, not a backend fetch.\nA separate frontend checkout may exercise those options only in synthetic/mock\npreviews and loopback local-configuration simulations; it does not authorize a\nreal backend fetch, provider access or production browser service.</p>\n<p>Hosted state includes authenticated profile-to-KMS/age associations,\nprotected keys and necessary enrollment, access-window, metering and billing\nrecords. Key bytes are not displayed or logged. Turning hosted use off via CLI\nor config before network leaves a fully client-side operation under its actual\nlocal policy/custody; it creates no hosted config record or subscription.</p>\n<h2 id=\"identity-and-authorization\">Identity and authorization</h2>\n<p>The identity key is the exact trusted issuer plus stable subject, not a username,\nemail address, or email-domain match. This follows <a href=\"https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html#ClaimStability\">OpenID Connect&#x27;s identity\nstability rules</a>.\nAn employer&#x27;s authorization domain and an enrolled application/client further\nconstrain the mapping. Authentication in domain A never unlocks B or C.</p>\n<p>Each configured token profile verifies signature, trusted issuer and keys,\nintended service audience, token type, validity, and required client/claim\nbindings. The API accepts access credentials for this service; an unrelated ID\ntoken or caller-selected signing URL is not equivalent. Explicit mappings turn\ntrusted scopes, roles, groups, or delegated authorization into particular\noperations. Unsupported profiles fail closed rather than guessing claim meaning.</p>\n<p>The effective permission is the intersection of the verified identity grant,\ncurrent service authorization, mapping scope, and active time window. A trusted\nIdP proves identity; it does not grant every authenticated account a key. UI,\nCLI, MCP, and direct API calls use the same server-side checks. A caller-supplied\nprincipal, tenant, mapping ID, or client label cannot establish ownership.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Authority</th>\n<th>Permitted responsibility</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity provider</td>\n<td>Authentication, MFA, account lifecycle, and its own groups/roles.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mapping administrator</td>\n<td>Manage authorized-domain key references and mappings within delegated scope. Administration does not implicitly permit key retrieval.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grant administrator</td>\n<td>Manage allowed principals, clients, operations, windows, and revocation within their own authority ceiling.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key reader</td>\n<td>Fetch only an explicitly authorized mapped key. Retrieval does not grant administrative authority.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auditor</td>\n<td>Read permitted metadata receipts without key or token contents.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are configurable capabilities, not fixed organization or environment\nnames. The UI shows effective access, its issuer/subject and client associations,\nits source, policy revision, timing, and revocation state. Updates use version\nchecks and server validation; browser controls are not the enforcement point.\nAdministrative audit records actor, recipient, mapping/version, changed scope,\nreason, decision, and time. They never contain private keys or bearer tokens.</p>\n<p>Service-side revocation fences new key-release decisions using current grant\nstate and a recheck before release. It does not recall an admitted response.\nIdP account/group changes have a separate propagation bound: locally verified\nJWTs can remain valid until expiry unless a configured introspection, session\nrevocation, or authenticated event mechanism establishes fresher state. The UI\nreports the applicable freshness bound instead of claiming immediate issuer\nrevocation from an old token. Strongly consistent permission reads prevent an\noutdated replica or cached allow from overriding an acknowledged service deny.</p>\n<h2 id=\"provisioning-exposure-and-issuer-response\">Provisioning, exposure and issuer response</h2>\n<p>The following integrations describe optional enterprise capabilities, not\nservices automatically enrolled or billed by the baseline key-retrieval plan.</p>\n<p>The service uses <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-configuration-governance&amp;part=part-1\">one governed configuration</a> for\ntenant defaults, locked controls, groups, contexts and effective workload rules.\nIts <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-credential-lifecycle&amp;part=part-1\">lifecycle contract</a> includes optional qualified\nSCIM provisioning/deprovisioning, independently authorized provider-native\nrotation/revocation, GitHub scanning and optional GitGuardian exposure policy.\nUI/API/CLI/SDK/MCP mutations use common validation, authorization, approval,\nrevision guards and required durable audit; an interface cannot widen a grant.</p>\n<p>Optional workload OIDC/SPIFFE, device-posture/Conditional Access and external\npolicy evaluators follow the same <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-credential-lifecycle&amp;part=part-1&amp;anchor=workload-identity-posture-and-external-decisions\">lifecycle profiles</a>,\nauthorization ceiling and forward migration contract. They cannot substitute\nfor tenant isolation, grant approval or required audit.</p>\n<p>Directory removal fences future access under the declared freshness profile.\nIncident closure cannot remove a known exposure block, and an exposure exception\ncannot override issuer revocation. Credential references remain protected and\nscanner/provider/directory credentials remain separate. Rotate application\ncredentials at their issuer when authorized; distributing a new age key alone\ndoes not invalidate credentials or ciphertext already accessible to a recipient.\nOTLP exports contain approved metadata only and do not make the audit store\nimmutable or recall released keys.</p>\n<p>Customers can use one self-contained configuration or explicitly compose\ntrusted departmental sources through a master under the\n<a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-configuration-governance&amp;part=part-1\">canonical policy contract</a>. Both share namespace,\nlocks, deterministic conflict resolution and complete validated activation.\nThe <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-configuration-repositories&amp;part=part-1&amp;anchor=corporate-github-and-personal-local-configuration\">corporate/personal composition case</a>\ndoes not enroll personal files, grants or decryption keys into the hosted\nservice. A remote service cannot resolve a client-local source by silently\nuploading it, exporting its provenance or treating omission as an authorized\npartial policy. Approved local resolution and optional domain-specific\ncache/refresh rules retain the same authority ceiling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"key-lookup-and-client-behavior\">Key lookup and client behavior</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">flowchart LR\n  I[Existing identity provider] --&gt;|Service access credential| C[Authenticated client]\n  C --&gt;|Exact mapped-key request| V[Verify identity and current permission]\n  U[Authorized administration UI / API] --&gt;|Versioned mappings and grants| V\n  V --&gt; K[Protected key storage]\n  K --&gt;|Mapped private key over authenticated TLS| C\n  C --&gt;|Key can decrypt matching ciphertext| B[Local encrypted bundle]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The request names a mapping, not an arbitrary secret-store path. The service\nchecks ownership before storage retrieval and releases one authorized key\nversion over authenticated TLS. Responses use <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code> and\nare excluded from CDN caching, request-body capture, traces, and error output.\nDurable required audit precedes release; audit failure denies the operation.\nAdministrative metadata responses do not contain private key bytes.</p>\n<p>Clients retain only their configured key profile and protected cache. A refresh\ninterval determines when a compliant client contacts the service again. Failed\nrefresh cannot extend an expired service grant or a protected local approval.\nA returned private key, however, has no intrinsic expiry: a recipient can copy\nit and use it independently. The service cannot prove that a client deleted it.</p>\n<p>Service profiles, issuer/client bindings, mapping references, refresh rules,\ncache/custody choice, and time bounds are explicit configuration. CLI CRUD\nmanages those declarations and doctor validates references and trust settings\nwithout displaying or fetching key material as a diagnostic side effect.\nBootstrap and approved workload operations obtain keys through the selected\nprofile. MCP administration uses the same scoped management operations; normal\nagent actions return task results. Age private keys remain bootstrap material,\nnot ordinary MCP secret aliases or model-visible management output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"distribution-local-custody-and-remote-unlocking\">Distribution, local custody, and remote unlocking</h2>\n<p>The hosted baseline now includes real KMS operations and age-private-key\ndistribution as distinct capabilities. Protected local custody and exact-bundle\nremote authorization still need separately implemented profiles. Do not\nsubstitute one capability for another.</p>\n<p>These are different security models. The age endpoint distributes private keys;\nthe KMS endpoint performs authorized cryptographic operations with remote master-key\ncustody. Neither alone provides every stronger custody boundary described below.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Where key material resides</th>\n<th>What revocation can stop</th>\n<th>Residual boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Private-key distribution</td>\n<td>The authorized client receives the age private key.</td>\n<td>Future fetches from the service.</td>\n<td>A copied key can decrypt old and future ciphertext addressed to that unchanged recipient. Refresh rules do not change this.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protected local custody</td>\n<td>An optional local service under a separate OS identity holds the key and performs approved operations.</td>\n<td>Future local operations while the service, OS, policy, and clock remain trusted.</td>\n<td>Same-user file hiding is insufficient. Root/Administrator, a compromised service, or captured plaintext/unwrapped data keys can bypass the boundary.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remote unlocking</td>\n<td>A separate remote custodian retains the private key and authorizes each exact bundle operation.</td>\n<td>Future remote unlock requests.</td>\n<td>A returned data key or plaintext can be retained. Remote private-key retention alone is not end-to-end recall. Fully offline use needs a separately trusted local enforcement boundary.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For protected custody, merely downloading a bundle grants no independent\nunlocking authority to the ordinary caller. The local service authenticates the\ncaller using OS or enrolled application identity, checks the exact authorization\ndomain, signed bundle digest, workload, permitted secrets, policy version, and\nwindow, and refreshes authorization before required key use. It offers bounded\noperations rather than a generic private-key export or unrestricted decrypt\noracle. It releases only permitted application values or launches the approved\nworkload. The service identity, files, IPC, process inspection rights, and\nadministrative update path form the local boundary.</p>\n<p>This custody claim does not apply once the ordinary caller receives a usable\nprivate key, whole-document data key, or plaintext. Hardware can reduce private\nkey export but does not prevent an authorized process retaining decrypted data.</p>\n"},{"id":"part-3","title":"Artifact and key isolation","html":"<h2 id=\"artifact-and-key-isolation\">Artifact and key isolation</h2>\n<p>Partition bundles and key authorities along real authorization boundaries.\nSecrets for employers A, B, and C do not share one decryptable bundle or one\nordinary age key. Per-field selection after whole-document decryption is\nminimization, not cryptographic isolation.</p>\n<p>Ordinary age recipients independently wrap the same file key, as specified by\nthe <a href=\"https://c2sp.org/age\">age format</a>. Every applicable recipient is an alternative\nunlock path. SOPS <a href=\"https://getsops.io/docs/usage/identities/key-groups/\">key groups</a>\ncan require a threshold of groups, but reconstruct the document data key; they\ndo not create per-field identity isolation. Recovery, local age, cloud KMS,\nTransit, plugins, and all group/threshold combinations belong in the authority\nreview. A bypass recipient defeats a mandatory online gate even if the normal\nclient uses the managed service.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Granularity</th>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>Revocation and recovery consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Per user/client/domain</td>\n<td>Smaller key-sharing scope and independently removable mappings.</td>\n<td>More recipients, key versions, and recovery operations; every remaining recipient still authorizes its bundle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Per organization/domain</td>\n<td>Fewer key objects and simpler distribution.</td>\n<td>Anyone who retained the shared key can decrypt ciphertext addressed to it; removing one mapping does not remove that person&#x27;s cryptographic access.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The configuration exposes this choice explicitly. An organization key is not\npresented as individually revocable cryptographic custody. Recovery authority\nis separately held, audited, and excluded from ordinary workload users.</p>\n<h2 id=\"signed-windows-and-offline-limits\">Signed windows and offline limits</h2>\n<p>SOPS and age encrypt data; they do not enforce decryption expiry. A signed\nwindow authenticates a deadline, while a trusted service or local boundary\nenforces it. The earliest applicable identity, local approval, offline, and\nprovider-credential deadline bounds new operations through that boundary.</p>\n<p>Protected clocks, rollback state, and monotonic checks support compliant local\nexecution. They do not mathematically guarantee offline expiry against a\nhostile administrator who controls the machine or retained copies. Offline\nlocal receipts are not authoritative central per-read audit.</p>\n<p>Revocation stops future authorized operations. It cannot recall plaintext,\nunwrapped data keys, a distributed age private key, or historical ciphertext.\nChanging an age recipient or rotating its key does not invalidate an old key\nplus an old encrypted copy. Fresh encryption/data keys protect future bundles;\nthe actual application credential needs issuer-side expiry, revocation, or\nrotation to contain an already disclosed credential.</p>\n<h2 id=\"established-integration-mechanisms\">Established integration mechanisms</h2>\n<p>CircularQuay reuses existing cryptography. These upstream mechanisms have\nseparate compatibility and custody boundaries; their existence is not a\nqualification receipt for a CircularQuay adapter.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Established capability and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SOPS age plugin support</td>\n<td>Upstream <a href=\"https://github.com/getsops/sops/blob/main/age/keysource.go\">age identity handling</a> recognizes plugin identities. Plugin executables and identities require explicit trusted installation, not arbitrary commands from downloaded policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SOPS key command</td>\n<td>The same upstream interface can obtain age private keys from a command. Because it returns keys, it is a distribution path, not proof of non-exportable custody.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SOPS key service</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://getsops.io/docs/usage/advanced/#using-sops-key-service\">SOPS documents</a> remote data-key operations without sending the master private key. Its transport has no built-in authentication/encryption; a protected transport and authorization boundary are required. Disable unintended local/key-service fallback.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exact-bundle authorization around a key service</td>\n<td>The <a href=\"https://github.com/getsops/sops/blob/main/keyservice/keyservice.proto\">upstream protobuf</a> carries a key identifier and data-key ciphertext, not a complete bundle/identity policy. A policy boundary must bind the authenticated request to the exact approved artifact before invoking it; bare key-service access is not that check.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>YubiKey/PIV</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/str4d/age-plugin-yubikey\">age-plugin-yubikey</a> keeps identity secret material in the token and supports PIN/touch policy. Presence requirements and caching affect unattended workloads; the token is not a remote revocation authority.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apple Secure Enclave</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/remko/age-plugin-se\">age-plugin-se</a> provides device-bound identities and biometric access controls on its supported Macs. It creates a hardware-specific recipient rather than converting any downloaded age private key into the same non-exportable key.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TPM</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Foxboron/age-plugin-tpm\">age-plugin-tpm</a> supports TPM-sealed age identities and optional PIN use. Device custody and recovery require their own policy; it does not establish employer revocation by itself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KMS or OpenBao/Vault Transit</td>\n<td>SOPS&#x27; existing online key backends can enforce provider-side unwrap authority. This is a different custody model from returning an age private key and retains that provider&#x27;s availability, audit, and lifecycle requirements.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"administration-ui-and-hosting-preference\">Administration UI and hosting preference</h2>\n<p>Reuse existing Cloudflare/Netlify authentication and administration surfaces\nwhere they fit. Production custom administration UI/screens remain unapproved\nwhile the functional baseline is stabilized. A separate frontend checkout may\nbuild <strong>synthetic/mock functional previews</strong> of user, account, operator and\nadministrator flows, including loopback dashboard local-configuration edits and\nsimulations. That narrow authorization is not a real backend, host proxy,\nprovider/key/account connection, production deployment, purchase or a new test\nsuite. The confirmed first-run CLI/text interface and authentication remain in\nscope. The UI controls below describe intended end-state possibilities, not a\ndelivered screen or production-operation approval.</p>\n<p>Reuse already available paid Netlify or Cloudflare capabilities before building\nlogin, account administration, policy screens, hosting, or observability.\nAn existing subscription is not assumed to include every identity, seat,\nretention, or API feature; enrollment records the actual entitlement and budget.\nNo additional purchase is implicit in this design.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reused platform surface</th>\n<th>Responsibility</th>\n<th>Application boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloudflare Access and its administration console</td>\n<td>Existing IdP connections, app access policies, session controls, and authentication audit.</td>\n<td>Validate the Access JWT under its declared profile and audience. Cloudflare account administration is not customer key-mapping authorization. See <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/access-controls/policies/\">Access policies</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/access-controls/applications/http-apps/authorization-cookie/validating-json/\">JWT validation</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Netlify Identity and its managed user administration</td>\n<td>Available application login, user/role lifecycle, function verification, and entitled identity audit.</td>\n<td>Use provider-managed authentication; do not add an email system. Identity roles gate app operations only after server verification. See <a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/manage/security/secure-access-to-sites/identity/overview/\">Identity capabilities</a> and <a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/manage/security/secure-access-to-sites/identity/manage-existing-users/\">managed user administration</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Netlify project protection/team SSO</td>\n<td>Protect the deployment and operator/team access where appropriate.</td>\n<td>These sessions are distinct from application-user identity and per-key grants; a shared site password cannot implement individual key revocation. See <a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/manage/security/secure-access-to-sites/overview/\">site access controls</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Production-unapproved custom key-specific administration UI</td>\n<td>Intended mapping metadata, scoped grants/windows, effective access, versioned changes, revocation and redacted audit.</td>\n<td>Synthetic/mock previews and loopback local-configuration simulations are authorized in the separate frontend checkout. They must not connect to a real backend, host proxy, provider, key or account. Production implementation remains unapproved; no key material in client bundles, public build variables or previews.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The same-origin UI/API pattern avoids an unnecessary cross-cloud identity and\nsecret path. Split hosting remains possible when a particular custody or\nenterprise identity requirement justifies it. Preview deployments use synthetic\nkeys and separate identities. Browser sessions, CSRF protection, CSP, origin\nchecks, request limits, abuse budgets, and server authorization protect changes;\nthe managed login screen alone does not authorize a mapping mutation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<h3 id=\"explicitly-confirmed-ui-design-and-bounded-preview-work\">Explicitly confirmed UI design and bounded preview work</h3>\n<p>The frontend development boundary covers synthetic user, account, operator and\nadministrator previews in a separate checkout, plus loopback dashboard\nconfiguration editing and simulations. This does <strong>not</strong> authorize a real backend or host proxy,\noperational keys, provider or account access, production deployment, purchase,\naccount setup, or a new test suite. Production custom UI operation remains\nunapproved until the functional baseline and its qualification are complete.</p>\n<p>The public landing is a single screen derived from the README&#x27;s reasoning:\n<strong>“Get the right secrets ready.”</strong> Use a concise, broadly appealing\nsummary of how CircularQuay connects existing secret providers to approved\napps, and a clear functionality diagram. Call out optional official SDKs,\nMCP, enterprise integration, SCIM and other independently approved features\nwithout implying that every integration is in the base hosted subscription.\nLogin offers only identity providers actually supported, configured and entitled\nby the chosen Cloudflare/Netlify deployment. A platform name or decorative\nprovider logo does not establish a working login option.</p>\n<p>After login, the client-side SPA reads one or multiple files/URLs through the\nuser&#x27;s own network/VPN and credentials, supports all configuration options,\nedits/resolves/validates and saves <strong>locally</strong>. Nothing sends config or source\ncredentials to a hosted backend/proxy/log/analytics path. Browser file grants\nand CORS are honored. An explicit authorized native CLI/SDK/local bridge may\nsupply equivalent access, never a hosted proxy or a silently reduced option set.\nThe backend provides authenticated profile→KMS/age associations and authorized\ncrypto operations or private-key retrieval, with enrollment, metering and billing; key values are protected and never\ndisplayed or logged.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">flowchart LR\n  R[Public landing: README reasoning and optional features] --&gt; I[Actually supported identity login]\n  I --&gt; C[Authenticated client SPA]\n  F[User files / URLs / VPN sources] --&gt;|User network and source credentials| C\n  C --&gt;|Resolve / edit / validate| L[Local save]\n  C --&gt;|Minimal authenticated profile request| K[Hosted KMS operations and age-key service]\n  K --&gt;|Authorized protected data-key operation| N[Established provider-native KMS]\n  K --&gt;|Protected authorized release; no display or logging| C\n  C &lt;--&gt;|User-authorized queries / client signals| O[User-selected telemetry endpoint]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The SPA may gather/display <strong>client OpenTelemetry</strong> only as user-configured.\nSelect the collector and, separately, its supported query/read endpoint,\npermissions and authentication. OTLP ingestion is not automatically a query\nAPI, and a browser cannot become a general OTLP receiver or reach a private/VPN\nendpoint merely because the URL was entered. Browser transport and exporters\nhave <a href=\"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/languages/js/exporters/\">platform/CORS limitations</a>.\nUse permitted browser fetch, user network access and explicit local/native\nintegration where supported. A local bridge needs its own enrollment,\nauthenticated caller/origin checks and bounded scope; it is not an unauthenticated\nloopback proxy or authority to inspect unrelated credentials.</p>\n<p>No hosted telemetry ingestion or dashboard store is implied. Queries stay on\nthe user&#x27;s client; read permissions do not imply collector-write permission or\naccess to another tenant. Only approved safe telemetry fields may be shown or\nemitted: no config content, source credentials, key values, tokens, raw provider\nbodies or sensitive error capture. Collector CORS/auth failures return honest\nunavailable/permission outcomes, not server relays. OTel export or a visible\nchart makes no immutable-audit or complete-history guarantee. Test data-flow,\nVPN/CORS/file/native boundaries and redaction before claiming a client feature\nimplemented; no browser/client qualification is established by this diagram.</p>\n<h2 id=\"local-development-integration-and-controlled-delivery\">Local development, integration, and controlled delivery</h2>\n<p>The managed service has one artifact, configuration, storage-migration, and\nauthorization contract across local development, CI, preview, and production.\nA developer instance is a loopback-only isolated deployment of that same\nversioned artifact. It uses an independent local data directory, isolated\ntenant/domain identifiers, synthetic keys and secrets, and a developer-approved\nidentity-provider profile. <code>development</code> and <code>production</code> are labels chosen by\nan operator; neither label grants an identity, selects a tenant, or weakens a\npolicy.</p>\n<p>Authentication and authorization remain real in a local instance. A local\nbrowser session, test header, loopback origin, or developer OS account is not a\nsubstitute for a verified service credential and server-side mapping decision.\nA self-hosted Keycloak fixture, a constrained wire-compatible identity fixture,\nand selected OpenBao scenarios provide repeatable integration inputs where they\nexercise their respective protocols. They use fabricated tenants, principals,\nrecipients, tokens, and encrypted values. They do not turn a test provider into\nan implicit production authority or require any particular IdP in a customer\ndeployment.</p>\n<p>Each local instance trusts only its own test certificate authority for its\ndeclared endpoints. Test certificates, issuer metadata, and isolated client\ntrust are created with the fixture and removed with it. The service never asks a\ndeveloper to add a permanent system/browser trust-store exception, disable TLS\nverification, or reuse an operational certificate. A trust failure is a test\nfailure, including on loopback.</p>\n<p>The same pinned artifact, policy fixtures, migration sequence, health contract,\nand redacted evidence format run locally and in CI. Cleanup removes only the\ntest instance&#x27;s data, certificates, and synthetic identities after evidence is\ncaptured; it does not mutate a shared provider, account, or host trust store.\nParallel runs use distinct instance, tenant, database, and audit namespaces.\nThis permits concurrency, retry, backup/restore, expiry, and revocation testing\nwithout conflating one developer&#x27;s result with another&#x27;s.</p>\n<p>Netlify Dev and Wrangler local adapters exercise the same authenticated API and\ntenant-isolation contract using synthetic inputs. They are adapter tests, not\nevidence that a hosted platform has enforced a production policy. Preview\nvalidation is separately scoped: it uses a preview-specific application,\nidentity audience, storage namespace, and synthetic key set, and it cannot read\nor modify production mappings, audit records, or custody material.</p>\n<p>The baseline qualification covers minimal registration/idempotency, mapping\nstorage/retrieval, authorization/windows, metering/no-charge behavior and the\napplicable isolation, privacy, durability and recovery controls. Optional\nenterprise profiles add their own tests only when selected; their costs and\nactivation are not assumed part of the subscription. The requirements below\nspan those separately qualified profiles.</p>\n<p>The required managed-service test gates cover exact authentication audience and\nissuer binding; tenant and authorization-domain isolation; operation scopes;\nmapping version conflicts; expiry and revocation before release; concurrent\ngrant/mapping updates; idempotent migration and rollback; backup encryption,\nrestore, and recovery authorization; durable audit-before-release; redaction;\nand health/recovery behavior. Provider-specific Keycloak, OpenBao, Netlify, and\nCloudflare routes add their own protocol and entitlement gates. A passing local\nfixture never substitutes for a native, provider, preview, or production\nqualification receipt; current results are recorded in the\n<a href=\"https://github.com/maldous/CircularQuay/blob/feature%2Finitial-implementation/docs/qualification.md#managed-service-design-status\" title=\"Repository reference (may differ from this local snapshot)\">qualification matrix</a>.</p>\n<p>Workload identity can strengthen service-to-service callers without replacing\nthe end-user/application authorization model. <a href=\"https://spiffe.io/docs/latest/spiffe-about/overview/\">SPIFFE</a>\nand <a href=\"https://spiffe.io/docs/latest/spire-about/spire-concepts/\">SPIRE</a> can supply\nworkload identities and short-lived mTLS credentials where an operator runs\nthem. Their trust domain, selectors, registration authority, workload attestation,\nand rotation/revocation behavior are explicit deployment configuration. A\nSPIFFE identity proves an enrolled workload identity; it does not itself select\na tenant, mapping, operation, or age key, and it does not replace an IdP access\ncredential when the policy requires one.</p>\n<p>Controlled delivery follows Gitflow: feature work integrates through <code>develop</code>,\na release branch stabilizes a candidate, <code>main</code> contains approved stable\nreleases, and a hotfix returns to every affected integration branch. Each v1\nrelease candidate and stable release is one immutable, versioned artifact set\nwith published digests and provenance. Rebuilding, rebadging, changing build\ninputs, or changing a migration produces a new candidate that repeats the\napplicable qualification; it cannot inherit approval from bytes that were not\ntested.</p>\n<p>Promotion uses the exact tested artifact in its approved environment. It\nrequires migration rehearsal and rollback evidence, health and readiness\nevidence, authorization/tenant-isolation verification, backup/restore evidence,\nand redacted audit evidence for the selected scope. A production promotion does\nnot create a broad publisher credential, bypass an environment approval, or\nmodify an unrelated instance. Publisher accounts, registry steps, and operator\ncommands are maintained in <a href=\"/explore#doc=release&amp;part=part-1\">RELEASE.md</a>; this document defines\nthe architecture and acceptance contract rather than an operator procedure.</p>\n"},{"id":"part-4","title":"Deployment state and prepared security settings","html":"<h2 id=\"deployment-state-and-prepared-security-settings\">Deployment state and prepared security settings</h2>\n<p>Repository source alone does not qualify a production deployment.\nThe inspected repository has no verified hosted-service\nCloudflare Worker/Netlify project identity, deployed endpoint, account-bound\nbindings or deployment configuration. That is evidence of <strong>design/source work,\nnot an inspected secured deployment</strong>; it does not assert that every external\naccount has been searched. No new account connection or deployment is implied.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Setting to prepare for an authorized target</th>\n<th>Verification required before calling it configured/tested</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>TLS-only service origin and exact resource/issuer audiences</td>\n<td>Read back actual endpoint/TLS policy, reject wrong issuer/audience/expired/malformed credentials and insecure redirect paths. Provider TLS is inherited only when the selected endpoint actually uses it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Encrypted durable age-key records</td>\n<td>Provider encryption at rest plus application-level envelope encryption through a maintained approved library/KMS profile. Bind ciphertext authentication to tenant/profile/key version/purpose; keep wrapping keys outside the database in separately scoped custody. Test wrong-domain decrypt denial and actual restore.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Separate signing custody only where needed</td>\n<td>Existing issuer signatures are verified; do not create an unnecessary token issuer/signing service. If service receipts require signing, use a distinct key/purpose/identity from encryption wrapping and rotate under explicit scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Least-privilege runtime/deployment identities</td>\n<td>Bind only the selected database and key-wrapping operations, separate runtime/publisher/recovery authority, no public database or broad account token. Read back grants and test cross-tenant/profile/resource denial.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private responses and secret-free operations</td>\n<td><code>Cache-Control: no-store</code> on key responses, no public-cache rule, no key/query/body/token capture in request logs/traces/error tools. Verify synthetic key markers never enter CDN/vendor logs, metrics or UI display.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Browser controls for future UI</td>\n<td>Restrictive CSP (<code>object-src &#x27;none&#x27;</code>, <code>frame-ancestors &#x27;none&#x27;</code>, controlled script sources/nonces), exact enrolled CORS origins and authenticated CSRF/session rules where relevant. CLI authentication does not depend on browser Origin. Client-selected config/query endpoints require explicit local origin/network policy, not <code>*</code> credentials or a backend proxy. Synthetic/mock previews and loopback</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>local-configuration simulations are authorized separately; production or</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>real-backend UI implementation remains unapproved.</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backup/restore/rotation and fail behavior</td>\n<td>Encrypt/export only necessary hosted records, preserve key/deny/usage versions, independently protect recovery/wrapping material and test restore before release. An application compromise can still use authorized unwrap paths: database encryption alone does not prevent it. Required unavailable custody/auth/ledger/audit denies release.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Record each control as <strong>inherited</strong>, <strong>explicitly configured</strong> or <strong>actually\ntested</strong>, with exact target identity, artifact/profile revision and receipt.\nThese prepared settings are not proof of any of those states, and no guessed\nWorker name, database ID, Netlify site or new deployment file is created to\nmake the service appear provisioned. The owner must identify an authorized\nexisting target before reversible live hardening can be performed; otherwise\nthe concrete settings and tests above remain the deployment preparation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"end-customer-accounts-subscriptions-and-support\">End-customer accounts, subscriptions and support</h2>\n<p>This is the explicitly added <strong>end-customer account service design</strong>, alongside\nthe small key-delivery API. Authenticated customers can inspect their account,\nprofile-attributed successful-fetch usage/history, invoices, plans/quotas,\nsubscription/entitlement status and support history. Customer lifecycle covers\nverified onboarding, association updates, suspension/offboarding, retention and\nseparately authorized financial reconciliation. It does not add hosted config\nstorage, provider-source credentials or custom UI implementation authority.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Customer-facing function</th>\n<th>Smallest established candidate</th>\n<th>What must not be confused with it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity/account login</td>\n<td>An actually entitled application-user IdP, for example <a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/manage/security/secure-access-to-sites/identity/plans-and-pricing/\">Netlify Identity</a> on supported credit plans, or a qualified B2C/OIDC issuer verified by a Worker.</td>\n<td>A Cloudflare/Netlify operator account, team SSO or dashboard token is not customer identity or permission to enumerate another customer&#x27;s records.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Successful-fetch usage/history</td>\n<td>The service&#x27;s durable profile-scoped event ledger and bounded authenticated query DTOs.</td>\n<td>Vendor request/compute dashboards and hosting invoices measure operator costs, not the customer&#x27;s logical successful fetches. Retry attempts and local key reuse have different semantics.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invoices, payment entry and subscription self-service</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.stripe.com/customer-management\">Stripe&#x27;s hosted customer portal</a> is one established candidate for customer payment details, invoices and allowed subscription actions; use the selected provider&#x27;s actually enabled feature set.</td>\n<td>Hosting-platform billing APIs charge the operator. They are not customer subscription/CRM services and their admin credentials never reach a customer or agent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Support/customer relationship</td>\n<td>Existing authorized support channel for a lean start; a separately selected service such as <a href=\"https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/pricing/\">Freshdesk</a> can supply customer ticket portal/shared inbox/history.</td>\n<td>Provider operator support tickets are not an end-customer helpdesk. Agent roles, SSO, history visibility, automation and any enterprise SLA need their exact support-plan entitlements.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Cloudflare/Netlify can host the authenticated adapter and its necessary account\nmetadata; neither is assumed to supply customer billing or CRM. Keep provider\ncustomer/subscription/invoice/ticket identifiers bound to the authoritative\ntenant/account, with field-scoped visibility. Never accept a client-supplied\nprovider customer ID as ownership. An account/email change cannot transfer\nanother tenant&#x27;s key, usage, subscription or support history. Profile count\nis metadata, not the usage unit. Key authorization/windows and billing\nentitlement are separate checks; payment never grants an otherwise denied key.</p>\n<p>Payment-card entry and sensitive payment-method management stay on the chosen\nprovider&#x27;s hosted pages. No card number, CVC, payment credential or provider\nadmin key is collected by the config client, model, support form or application\nlogs. Portal sessions are short-lived and created only for the verified\ncustomer; redirect/deliver them through the trusted client rather than logging\nbearer-like URLs or dumping them into model-visible diagnostics. Reuse existing\nprovider portals instead of implementing deferred custom account/payment screens.</p>\n<p>CLI/API/SDK/MCP operations share the same current tenant/resource/field scopes,\nvalidation, approvals, idempotency, durable audit and safe errors. Read account,\nusage or invoice status only for the authorized customer. Plan changes,\ncancellation, refund, invoice/payment action and support/customer mutations are\ndistinct operations; service capability <strong>never authorizes an automatic charge\nor upgrade</strong>. Future unattended financial work needs an explicit user-approved\nboundary naming action, account/tenant, plan, currency/amount/budget, period,\nexpiry and approval/confirmation requirements. Missing, ambiguous or expired\nauthority denies or requests the applicable confirmation. A standing grant does\nnot override a required per-action confirmation, nor justify a workaround through\na payment-provider admin API.</p>\n<p>Reconcile provider state through authenticated APIs and verified signed events,\nnot a browser success redirect, a client-provided paid flag or webhook arrival\norder. <a href=\"https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks\">Stripe webhook guidance</a> requires a\nqualified signature/event profile; deduplicate deliveries and reconcile current\nobject state so stale events cannot reactivate old entitlement. Bind local\noperation idempotency to the exact customer, intent and parameters; provider\n<a href=\"https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests\">idempotency semantics</a> have\nAPI/version/retention limits and do not replace the local durable ledger.\nTimeout, duplicate event or provider outage cannot create repeated invoices,\ncharges, upgrades, grants or meter entries. Return safe operation state and\nreconcile uncertain effects before retry.</p>\n<p>Keep necessary account/usage/billing/support metadata in protected isolated\nstorage with declared retention/access/export/deletion rules and a bounded\ncustomer history. Provider invoices and retained financial records are not\narbitrarily erased by ordinary offboarding. Support intake accepts only approved\nsynthetic/structured diagnostics; no config/source-credential attachments,\nprivate keys, raw environment dumps or automatic log upload. Customer portal,\nsupport SSO/API automation and machine billing actions need synthetic positive/\nnegative/tenant/duplicate/out-of-order/confirmation tests and real capability\nqualification before availability is claimed. Nothing here connects a provider,\ncreates a live account, purchases a plan or authorizes financial execution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"account-rbac-and-scoped-authority\">Account RBAC and scoped authority</h2>\n<p>Server-side <strong>account-policy metadata</strong> governs access to account/service\nresources; it is distinct from client configuration, which stays local. Model\nan auditable human or nonhuman principal, role permissions, a separate scope\nassignment, conditions/time bounds and delegation source. Scope can constrain\ntenant, organization, customer subset, group, environment, explicitly selected\nfleet, key <strong>metadata</strong> and billing objects. A role name or UI persona never\nselects a tenant or supplies ownership.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role/persona</th>\n<th>Permitted responsibilities when assigned</th>\n<th>Default scope and exclusions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>One global superuser administrator</td>\n<td>Protected global authority over application account policy, roles/scope assignment, product/service administration and recovery control.</td>\n<td><strong>Exactly one active application superuser account.</strong> Global is intentional, not ordinary support. It does not imply plaintext key release, client config access or unrestricted financial execution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Product owner</td>\n<td>Product/plan/service policy proposals and approved account-lifecycle administration within assigned authority.</td>\n<td>No customer-wide browse, financial action or production change merely from the title. Global tasks need explicit assignment from the superuser&#x27;s policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer/account owner</td>\n<td>Own account metadata, profile usage/history, permitted invoices/subscription and support actions.</td>\n<td>Own verified account/tenant; payment changes still need explicit financial authority/confirmation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer enterprise administrator</td>\n<td>Authorized enterprise account/group associations and profile-bounded client-side group/config/selected-fleet operations below.</td>\n<td>Only assigned organization/customer/group/environment/fleet resources; never the provider support plane or a hosted config store.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Individually assigned support person</td>\n<td>Safe account/key metadata, own-assigned tickets, approved activity/utilization/geography and bounded investigation.</td>\n<td>Specified enterprise organizations and/or customer subsets, not all customers by default; no routine key bytes, payment mutation, source credentials or fleet execution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Billing/finance role</td>\n<td>Authorized invoice/subscription review and separately approved scoped financial mutations.</td>\n<td>Assigned customer/billing objects and action/amount/currency/time; not global provider-admin tokens or automatic charge authority.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service/infrastructure operator</td>\n<td>Scoped health/telemetry, safe maintenance, protected deployment/storage operations.</td>\n<td>Named service/resources/environments; support authority and financial authority are not inherited.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security auditor</td>\n<td>Authorized activity, access-policy and integrity/retention evidence review.</td>\n<td>Read-only assigned scopes; audit export has separate permission and no secret/config contents.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recovery custodian</td>\n<td>Explicit recovery/rotation ceremony using independently protected material.</td>\n<td>Recovery purpose/time; not a second standing global superuser or normal key-reader account.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI agent/service principal</td>\n<td>Named nonhuman identity performs the explicitly delegated subset of the above operations.</td>\n<td>Bound sponsor, tenant/resources/actions/deadline/budget; no shared human login, implied global role or confirmation bypass.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Each assignment binds <strong>role + scope + conditions</strong> as one tuple. Evaluate the\nrequested action/resource against each active matching tuple, then intersect\nwith verified client/profile/provider/delegation authority and mandatory safety\nchecks. Do not union permissions from one assignment with scopes from another\nand accidentally grant write access to the other&#x27;s customers. Applicable\nexplicit denies win; security floors combine restrictively, time bounds\nintersect and unresolved conflicts deny. Unknown roles/resources/groups and\nambiguous tenant ownership grant nothing. Explain only viewer-authorized role/\nscope provenance, never another customer&#x27;s metadata or client config.</p>\n<p>Support assignments are personal, not shared: a synthetic support principal\nassigned to organization A/customers A1/A2 cannot read A3 or B merely because\nits role allows support reads. A separate assignment can authorize a different\nsubset and action. Delegation names grantor/recipient, exact role-action/scope,\nreason, approvals, time and parent authority; it cannot widen the grantor,\nremove a deny, enable self-approval or become an indefinite group grant.\nChanging membership/scope/revocation invalidates affected sessions/approvals/\nqueued operations and rechecks generation after waits/before effects across\nUI/API/CLI/SDK/MCP. Revocation stops future access, not copied key/plaintext recall.</p>\n<p>The single global superuser uses a uniquely identified protected application\naccount with qualified strong MFA/possession, short sessions, step-up and\nredacted audit of privileged reads/mutations. Routine work uses separately\nscoped identities; credentials are never shared with support or AI agents.\nCreate/replace/recover that one principal through an explicit controlled\nceremony with independently protected recovery, concurrency/invariant checks\nand audit. Recovery cannot leave two active superusers or quietly mint another\nstanding global administrator. Provider account owners remain separate\ninfrastructure identities, not customer-facing application superusers.</p>\n<p>Global authority is real for account administration but is not a bypass for\nsignature/tenant/custody/audit/financial-confirmation requirements. No role\nassignment, including superuser, automatically grants age-private-key retrieval\nor operator plaintext display. A key release still needs its exact authorized\nprofile/mapping/window; custody recovery is a separate scoped operation.\nA privileged application/provider compromise remains a residual risk, not\nsomething a role label makes impossible. No live role, user or privilege is\ncreated by this design.</p>\n<h2 id=\"customer-enterprise-administration-and-selected-fleet\">Customer enterprise administration and selected fleet</h2>\n<p>Customer enterprise administrators act <strong>exclusively within the authorizing\nprofile</strong>, intersected with verified IdP/provider scopes, tenant ownership and\nexplicit selected-fleet authority. They are not provider support operators.\nTheir client may inspect/change authorized group associations, client config\nand selected fleet actions only through actual qualified provider/agent\ncapabilities. Group membership or an “enterprise” subscription label alone is\nnot permission to administer another organization or every host.</p>\n<p>All configuration stays on the customer client. A remotely hosted UI executes\nconfiguration/group/source work in its browser/native client through that\nuser&#x27;s VPN/network/credentials; it never uploads config/source credentials to\nthe hosted key service. Browser CORS, file permissions and provider public-client\nOAuth capabilities are real constraints. Use an explicitly authorized native\nCLI/SDK/local integration when needed; do not proxy VPN/provider credentials\nthrough a backend or invent a provider group-write interface.</p>\n<p>Selected fleet operations bind immutable target IDs, action/input constraints,\nactor/profile, exact tenant/environment, approvals and time. Require an explicit\nbounded selection; no implicit all-fleet wildcard, guessed URL or profile-name\nimpersonation. Each qualified agent/provider verifies target authorization and\ncurrent policy at dispatch/result/cancel, reconciles idempotent jobs and reports\npartial host failures without claiming cross-fleet atomicity. Provider support\ncannot impersonate this role from a ticket. No deployed fleet-agent/control\nentry point was verified in the inspected native source, so this is a design\ncapability/dependency, not a new working command or permission for live actions.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Proposed operation</th>\n<th>Client-only option / unresolved conflict</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct reachable group/config management</td>\n<td>Use the user&#x27;s actually authorized provider endpoint or local native client, validate locally and save locally; verify actual CORS/VPN/auth/API scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct selected-agent operations</td>\n<td>Only where an enrolled reachable client/agent capability exists, authenticate target/profile and keep config on the selected customer clients. Agent protocol, local storage, offline handling and permissions require implementation/evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Existing customer fleet-management service</td>\n<td>Use only explicitly authorized metadata/actions through its actual API; if it requires storing desired config or credentials centrally, expose that conflict and obtain an explicit design decision rather than silently doing it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Always-on fleet broker/inventory or offline convergence</td>\n<td>Requires state/connectivity/agent infrastructure beyond the key/account service; not automatically solved by a browser. Prefer customer-owned local state/direct execution or report unavailable. A hosted config/credential store would violate the current boundary and is not authorized.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Fleet/provider licensing, agent lifecycle/distribution, connectivity, enterprise\nIdP scopes, operator oversight and retention are optional separately measured\ncosts, not hidden in the successful-fetch price. Key fetches remain the usage\nunit; an “enterprise volume” fetch bundle does not grant group/fleet management.\nThe intended UI/API/CLI/SDK/MCP share scoped rules, but expose only actually\nimplemented, reachable and authorized operations. Production custom UI operation remains unapproved and no fleet/provider action\nis authorized by this design. Synthetic/mock frontend previews retain the same\nno-backend, no-provider, no-key and no-account boundary.</p>\n"},{"id":"part-5","title":"Restricted remotely hosted operator administration","html":"<h2 id=\"restricted-remotely-hosted-operator-administration\">Restricted remotely hosted operator administration</h2>\n<p>The operator console is a <strong>distinct restricted persona/plane</strong>, not the public\nlanding, customer billing portal or client configuration SPA. It is a confirmed\ndesign for customer/account metadata, safe database operations, support/root-cause\ninvestigation, service monitoring, infrastructure maintenance and telemetry.\nReuse existing hosting, payment, helpdesk and monitoring consoles before building\nduplicate screens. A separate frontend checkout may preview the console with\nsynthetic/mock data and loopback simulations; production console operation\nremains unapproved until the baseline is stable. This design authorizes no live\ncustomer-data access, production mutation, deployment, purchase or account\nconnection.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operator view/action</th>\n<th>Allowed boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Profile/key support metadata</td>\n<td>Authorized tenant/profile scope can inspect key ID, public age recipient, public-recipient fingerprint, version, status and relevant timestamps. No routine plaintext private-key display, decrypted bundle or private-key hash. A public fingerprint is not a secret-value hash or proof of ownership.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Account/security activity</td>\n<td>Bounded records identify source/channel, UTC time, action, safe result and opaque request correlation; include only necessary authorized actor/resource metadata. Source means service/channel provenance, not a client config URL, source credential or uploaded configuration. Operator reads and exports themselves need scope and audit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Utilization/history</td>\n<td>Reconciled successful-fetch events, quota/subscription outcome and duplicate/failed-attempt distinctions, never key contents. Payment-provider state remains independently reconciled.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Geography</td>\n<td>Coarse country/region or service-edge/processing location only when justified for security/support. Label network-derived context as approximate, not physical-person proof. Minimize precision, restrict access and declare retention/export/privacy controls; raw IP/device/location data is not routine dashboard enrichment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safe database/maintenance</td>\n<td>Read-only metadata by default; approved parameterized/allowlisted operations, bounded scans, dry-run/version guards and audited scoped mutations. Backup/restore/migration/rotation/repair uses separate privileges and tested recovery; no generic SQL/credential shell, secret dump or silent cross-tenant write.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring/infrastructure</td>\n<td>Service-owned latency/error/abuse/custody/backup/usage signals and actual resource health. Not an ingestion path for client configuration/source credentials or arbitrary client OTel. Financial and infrastructure changes need their own authority; support access is not permission to change a plan or restart production.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Require strong operator authentication, least-privilege tenant/resource/field\nroles, short sessions, step-up/independent approval where declared, and redacted\naudit of changes and privileged reads. Separate ordinary support, billing,\nsecurity, infrastructure and recovery authority. Break-glass is explicitly\ncontrolled and audited, not a path to routine key display. Provider dashboards\nremain operator-only: <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/manage-members/roles/\">Cloudflare roles</a>\nand <a href=\"https://docs.stripe.com/get-started/account/teams/roles\">Stripe team roles</a>\nshow why “administrator” is too broad for ordinary support. Verify actual\nNetlify/hosting/helpdesk role/audit/SSO entitlements before claiming them.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-credential-boundaries&amp;part=part-1&amp;anchor=hosted-database-protection-and-compromise-boundaries\">database protection and compromise contract</a>\nmaps Cloudflare or Netlify native controls, configuration, plan/cost limits and\nrequired tests for key custody <strong>and account/CRM/admin data</strong>. Select one provider\nagainst the actual profile; the authorized launch uses protected software keys\noutside DB plus vetted crypto, not paid external KMS. The runtime/shared-key risk\nis explicit; future managed/HSM custody needs a separate funded qualification.\nProvider at-rest encryption plus correctly implemented application envelope\nencryption is the proposed protection for covered stored fields; it is not yet\nverified in a hosted implementation. An application/operator compromise with unwrap authority can\nstill abuse that access. A console label cannot guarantee isolation from a\nprivileged provider administrator. Restrict and test custody paths, recovery\nidentities, logging and field-level redaction instead of claiming impossibility.\nISO/IEC 27001 alignment is a design/evidence goal, <strong>not certification</strong>, and a\nlog view is not immutable audit. No new custom console or live-data test has\nbeen implemented by documenting this design.</p>\n<h2 id=\"profile-utilization-and-fair-metering\">Profile utilization and fair metering</h2>\n<p>A use in the original age-retrieval meter is one <strong>hosted age-key access event\nattributed to a profile</strong>. Genuine KMS operations now need separately typed\nlogical usage and an explicit quota/tariff decision; automatic dual association\ncreation is not automatically either billable event. Do not equate native KMS\nAPI attempts with customer logical uses. The original age unit is not\na profile, key slot, configured machine or local decrypt. One daily hosted fetch\nis one use that day; twenty hosts making twenty independent hosted fetches are\ntwenty uses. Reusing an already held local key without contacting the hosted\nkey service adds zero hosted uses. This metric does not imply that the service\ncan observe or charge for offline decryption.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Outcome</th>\n<th>Customer utilization</th>\n<th>Provider cost / safety treatment</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Authorized logical access reaches the durable key-release decision</td>\n<td>One use, committed once with its operation ID.</td>\n<td>Current mapping/window checks and required receipt precede release; no key bytes in the meter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatic retry/reconnect of that same logical access</td>\n<td>No second use.</td>\n<td>Same principal/client/profile/key version, request binding and idempotency identity; bounded reconciliation and current authorization still required.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent fetch by another host, or a new logical access</td>\n<td>A separate use on success.</td>\n<td>One host cannot reuse another client&#x27;s operation ID to share a billed authorization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invalid/denied/expired request or failed access before release commit</td>\n<td>Zero customer uses.</td>\n<td>Requests/CPU/abuse control and safe denial logging can still cost the operator.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network loss after release commit; delivery uncertain</td>\n<td>At most that one committed use, pending reconciliation.</td>\n<td>Do not pretend TCP receipt proves client consumption. Reconcile safe operation state; do not charge each retry. Support an audited correction for proven service failure under the eventual billing policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local cached-key reuse, registration, usage inspection, offboarding/revocation</td>\n<td>Zero hosted retrieval uses.</td>\n<td>Keep these operational/security actions separately counted internally; they are not extra retrieval charges.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Bind the durable event to tenant, profile, verified principal/client, immutable\nkey version, request scope and finite idempotency window. Reusing an ID with\ndifferent binding rejects. Define retention/retry limits in the versioned\ncontract; no indefinite secret-response cache or arbitrary cross-host replay.\nLost metering/audit state must not yield a free unrecorded release or duplicate\ncharge. SDK/MCP/API/CLI use the same logical-access semantics. Do not hash the\nprivate key to create a meter or upload personal configuration for attribution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"production-protection-and-service-objectives\">Production protection and service objectives</h2>\n<p>Enterprise production quality applies to the <strong>same small profile-to-key API</strong>,\nnot a new configuration backend or UI. For the long-term enterprise profile, qualify the exact\ninfrastructure/entitlements, least-privilege deployment credentials,\nheadless-compatible abuse controls, encrypted durable state, consistency,\nrecovery and geography. No plan label, global CDN or published SLA proves this\napplication meets its objectives. Production custom UI remains unapproved;\nsynthetic/mock previews retain their no-backend/no-provider/no-key boundary,\nand all configuration remains client-side.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Control</th>\n<th>Required behavior and platform evidence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DDoS/WAF/bot protection</td>\n<td>Both vendors document platform DDoS protection. <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/waf/feature-interoperability/\">Cloudflare security phases</a> distinguish skippable SBFM from Free Bot Fight Mode, which cannot be selectively skipped. Do not put indiscriminate browser challenges on authenticated CLI/CI fetches. <a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/manage/security/secure-access-to-sites/web-application-firewall/\">Netlify WAF</a> requires Enterprise + High-Performance Edge; Pro is not equivalent. Rule tuning, false-positive and abuse tests are mandatory.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rate limits</td>\n<td>Apply coarse unauthenticated per-IP cost protection and independently authenticated per-client/profile/tenant limits with finite bursts and retry guidance. NAT/shared-IP users need fair treatment. <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/rate-limit/\">Workers limiter</a> is approximate/per-location, not a global accounting guarantee. Use qualified strongly consistent admission/ledger state for strict limits. <a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/manage/security/secure-access-to-sites/rate-limiting/\">Netlify limits</a> vary by plan; full controls need HP Edge. No unverified request header is an identity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authentication</td>\n<td>Exact issuer/resource/client/profile binding, current windows and revocation before release, short-lived credentials and replay protection. Automated clients receive structured denial/429, not CAPTCHA/JavaScript challenges or a weakened bypass. Operator/site SSO is not key-release authority.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protected state</td>\n<td>Platform TLS and encryption at rest are necessary. <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/reference/data-security/\">D1 security</a> does not imply application-owner HSM custody. Use approved key wrapping/domain isolation, least-privilege storage access and protected rotations; record wrapping/restore dependencies and costs. Never store client config or source credentials.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consistency</td>\n<td>Commit authorization generation, required usage/idempotency/audit outcome and revocation fence before release, with recheck after waits. <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/best-practices/read-replication/\">D1 replication sessions</a> do not by themselves make every replica globally current. Critical decisions must use qualified authoritative/transactional state; do not use eventual KV/edge cache for current release grants.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Residency and placement</td>\n<td>Enroll the supported storage jurisdiction/region and processing/log/backup boundaries, not an inferred CDN location. Placement hints are not contractual residency. Test primary-region latency and failover against those constraints; do not replicate private keys into an undeclared region for speed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recovery and rotation</td>\n<td>Rehearse encrypted backups and independent custody recovery, key-wrapping/signing credential rotation, revocation/meter history preservation and restore fencing. <a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/build/data-and-storage/netlify-database/backup-and-recovery/\">Netlify backups</a> have plan-specific retention and restoring loses post-backup changes. Never restore a grant or double-charge by dropping later deny/usage state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache and failure</td>\n<td>Key responses use <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code> and are excluded from public CDN/application caches, request/response capture and debug tools. Fail closed on unavailable required authorization, authoritative quota or audit; no cached key-response fallback. Reconcile uncertain release rather than repeatedly charging or minting credentials.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operations</td>\n<td>Secret-free metrics/logs/traces, low-cardinality outcome/duration/limiter-health signals, alerts and bounded operator response. Key values/tokens are neither displayed nor logged; WAF/vendor telemetry also needs payload-capture review. Operational export is not immutable audit.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Initial <strong>design objectives, not measured results or contractual promises</strong>:\n99.9% successful authorized-fetch availability over a rolling 30-day window\n(43.2 minutes equivalent error budget), with errors from required storage/IdP/\nprotection dependencies counted. Track policy/abuse denials separately, and\ncount erroneous blocking/challenges of valid headless requests as service errors.\nMeasure both endpoint and synthetic authenticated user-path availability; a\n200 health check alone is insufficient. Higher contractual targets require a\nqualified priced topology, not an assumed free-tier guarantee.</p>\n<p>For warm authenticated fetches with cached valid issuer metadata, target p95\nunder 500 ms/p99 under 1 second from the enrolled primary geography; across\ncontinents target p95 under 1.2 seconds/p99 under 2 seconds. Report cold start,\ninteractive login, throttling and dependency-outage results separately. Test\nSydney, Singapore, a US region and a European region, with at least 1,000\nsynthetic fetches/location under ordinary and burst load, TLS, correct denial,\nkey-window expiry, rotation and regional/provider failure. Confirm DNS/route/\nregion/residency and record the actual percentiles, failures and exact artifact.\nThese targets may need revision from measurements; no low-latency/absolute-HA\nclaim or geography is established by documentation.</p>\n<p>Recovery objectives for qualification are a 60-minute service RTO and at most\n15-minute key/mapping RPO, with <strong>no admission of lost/uncertain revocation or\nusage history</strong>: fence retrieval until reconciled even if this extends outage.\nDaily backups alone cannot meet that RPO. Price the needed incremental\nreplication/journal/backup retention and independent restore tests before\nclaiming the objective met. Free tiers are development comparisons, not\ninterchangeable production guarantees.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hosting-economics-and-capacity-assumptions\">Hosting economics and capacity assumptions</h2>\n<p>This is <strong>out-of-pocket Cloudflare/Netlify hosting only</strong>, in USD, checked\n4 November 2026. <strong>Labor is removed entirely.</strong> No AWS/external KMS, CRM/helpdesk,\npayment fees, profit margin or customer tariff is embedded. Prior labor-loaded\nreview prices are not hosting costs and are superseded. The SAME existing\nforecast-calculation.json, plan-proposal-calculation.json and verify-forecast.py\nnow contain this analysis; historical receipts preserve earlier work only.</p>\n<p>Personal, Business and Enterprise below are <strong>customer product-tier workload\nexamples</strong>, not hosting plans. An Enterprise customer does not require Netlify\nEnterprise or a Cloudflare Enterprise contract merely because of its name.\nProduct limits/prices are not approved here. Actual existing paid tier/project,\nremaining included allowances and customer/workload identity are unverified:\nthese are conditional public-plan examples, not your observed bill or permission\nto buy packs, change plans, connect accounts or deploy.</p>\n<h3 id=\"hosting-only-answer-for-the-three-product-workloads\">Hosting-only answer for the three product workloads</h3>\n<p>One paying customer plus one free owner fits the illustrated $5 Workers Paid\nbase or $9 Netlify Personal base at 15 active DB unit-hours/month for <strong>all three\nproduct workloads</strong>. If those plans are already paid and sufficient allowances\nremain, incremental spend is $0. The key variable on Netlify is how long the DB\nstays active, not whether the customer is called Personal or Enterprise.</p>\n<p>For a useful scale comparison, this table is <strong>100 paying customers all on the\nsame product tier, plus 100 free owners</strong>. Per payer: Personal 300, Business\n3,000, Enterprise 20,000 combined successful software wrap/unwrap and age fetches\nper month; 1/3/10 profiles. Free owner: one profile and 30 combined successes.\nIllustrative mix is 75% software crypto, 25% age fetches. Each wrap or unwrap is\none logical operation; creation/retry/local reuse is not automatically another\nbillable success. These are sizing inputs, not an approved subscription offer.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Product workload, 100 payers + 100 free</th>\n<th>CF total allocated / extra above already-paid $5</th>\n<th>Netlify Personal: total / extra above already-paid $9</th>\n<th>Netlify Pro: total / extra above already-paid $20</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Personal; DB 15 active unit-hours</td>\n<td>$5.00 / $0.00</td>\n<td>$9 / $0</td>\n<td>$20 / $0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personal; DB 720 active unit-hours</td>\n<td>$5.00 / $0.00</td>\n<td>$74 / $65</td>\n<td>$50 / $30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business; DB 15 active unit-hours</td>\n<td>$5.00 / $0.00</td>\n<td>$9 / $0</td>\n<td>$20 / $0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business; DB 720 active unit-hours</td>\n<td>$5.00 / $0.00</td>\n<td>$74 / $65</td>\n<td>$50 / $30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise product; DB 15 active unit-hours</td>\n<td>$5.33 / $0.33</td>\n<td>$19 / $10</td>\n<td>$20 / $0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise product; DB 720 active unit-hours</td>\n<td>$5.33 / $0.33</td>\n<td>$89 / $80</td>\n<td>$60 / $40</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>All Netlify figures above must add its actual database-storage charge</strong>, whose\ncurrent numeric tariff was not supplied on the checked official page. It is\nunknown, not assumed free and not replaced with a made-up rate. CF includes D1\nand the illustrated encrypted R2 recovery/archive overage. The app is assumed to\nhave the entire published included allowances unused; other apps&#x27; consumption\ncan increase incremental spend. This is a shared hosting bill for the cohort,\nnot a per-customer hosting subscription. Do not multiply the $5/$9 base fee by\ncustomer count.</p>\n<p>Netlify live storage in the 100-payer examples is <strong>0.058902528 GB Personal,\n0.491339264 GB Business, 3.195472384 GB Enterprise product</strong>. The official\n<a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/build/data-and-storage/netlify-database/billing-and-usage/\">DB billing page</a>\nstill says storage billing starts no earlier than 1 July 2026 and rates will be\nannounced, but supplies no current numeric storage rate on this checked date.\nThe Netlify totals are therefore <strong>bounded partial meter totals</strong>, not all-in\nfigures. If a later verified quote is r USD/GB-month, the storage sensitivity is\nr × those quantities; for example hypothetical r=$1 adds $0.06/$0.49/$3.20.\nThat $1 is an illustration, <strong>not a Netlify rate</strong>. If storage instead consumes\ncredits, add its actual credits before pack rounding rather than blindly adding\nUSD. This unknown must be resolved from authorized official/account evidence.</p>\n<p>The 15-hour branch means bursty/scheduled use keeping one DB compute unit active\nfor 15 hours in the month. The 720-hour branch means one unit continuously active\nfor a 30-day month. <a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/build/data-and-storage/netlify-database/configure-sleep-on-inactivity/\">Netlify DB sleep</a>\ndefaults to five idle minutes; regular requests or background jobs can keep it\nawake. Measure active unit-hours; do not claim the cheap bursty branch for steady\ntraffic. Other credit plans/legacy terms, actual region/currency and feature\nrequirements can differ. No exchange-rate conversion is used.</p>\n<h3 id=\"current-official-rates-and-included-limits\">Current official rates and included limits</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Meter</th>\n<th>Published rate/input used</th>\n<th>Inflection</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/\">Workers Paid</a></td>\n<td>$5/month, 10M requests + 30M CPU-ms included; excess $0.30/M requests and $0.02/M CPU-ms; no Workers bandwidth egress fee.</td>\n<td>CPU or requests can add usage charges without buying a higher named hosting tier.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/pricing/\">D1 Paid</a></td>\n<td>25B reads, 50M writes, 5 GB included; excess $0.001/M reads, $1/M writes, $0.75/GB-month. No hourly DB compute bill.</td>\n<td>Physical index writes count; no scans-as-one-read shortcut. 10 GB/database cap needs a qualified sharding design, not just paying storage overage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Workers Logs</td>\n<td>On this model date 20M events included, then $0.60/M; operational retention is bounded.</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/observability/pricing/\">Announced observability pricing</a> changes from 1 December 2026; rerun then. Logs do not replace durable required audit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/pricing/\">R2 Standard</a></td>\n<td>If unused: 10 GB-month, 1M Class A writes, 10M Class B reads included. Excess $0.015/GB-month, $4.50/M Class A, $0.36/M Class B; upward billing-unit rounding, no egress fee.</td>\n<td>Included allowance assumed available <strong>only for this conditional example</strong>. Unknown already-used allowance is not free capacity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.netlify.com/pricing/\">Netlify public plans</a></td>\n<td>USD examples: Personal $9/1,000 credits, extra $5/500; Pro $20/3,000, extra $10/1,500.</td>\n<td>Actual existing plan/currency/features must be read back. Packs/auto-recharge/upgrade are not authorized by this model.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/manage/accounts-and-billing/billing/billing-for-credit-based-plans/how-credits-work/\">Netlify credit meters</a></td>\n<td>2 credits/10,000 requests; 10/GB-hour Functions compute; 20/GB web + database egress; 15/successful production deploy. Four production deploys/month = 60 credits.</td>\n<td>Requests, compute, bandwidth, DB and deployments all consume the same allowance. Published public-plan examples are not actual unused account credits.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/build/data-and-storage/netlify-database/billing-and-usage/\">Netlify DB</a></td>\n<td>10 credits/active compute-unit-hour; minimum 1 unit when awake. 20 credits/GB DB egress. Current storage tariff unresolved on checked page.</td>\n<td>720 hours = 7,200 credits <strong>before app traffic</strong>; one unit at 100 h exhausts Personal&#x27;s 1,000 credits before other uses, 300 h exhausts Pro&#x27;s 3,000. Personal/Pro DB limits and backup retention still apply.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/manage/security/secure-access-to-sites/identity/plans-and-pricing/\">Netlify Identity</a></td>\n<td>Included on eligible credit plans, no separate paid IdP assumed. CF requires an actually qualified existing/free issuer.</td>\n<td>Validate machine/human/sender/MFA and scoped grants; a dashboard account is not customer key access. Missing entitlement/identity makes a branch unavailable.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"inflection-points-to-watch\">Inflection points to watch</h3>\n<p><strong>Netlify:</strong> under these public pack prices, Personal is cheaper up through\n2,000 credits; above 2,000, base Pro plus packs is cheaper than Personal plus\npacks, assuming both are feature-eligible. That is a cost comparison, not upgrade\npermission; higher Pro credit bundles may alter the cheapest purchase choice.\nAt 720 active DB unit-hours the minimum starts around <strong>$74 on Personal or $50\non Pro</strong> before significant request traffic and unknown storage. Sleep behavior\ncan therefore change the bill much more than a few thousand crypto calls.\nA minimum-tier launch must bound admission at its actual credit/feature limits;\nno automatic recharge or purchase is implied.</p>\n<p><strong>Cloudflare:</strong> first included-allowance crossings under the exact homogeneous\nproduct/cohort assumptions above (one free owner per payer; all unused allowance\nassigned to this app) are:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First crossing, paying product customers</th>\n<th>Personal</th>\n<th>Business</th>\n<th>Enterprise product</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>R2 archive/recovery exceeds 10 GB</td>\n<td>1,903</td>\n<td>213</td>\n<td>33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Workers CPU exceeds 30M ms</td>\n<td>6,058</td>\n<td>667</td>\n<td>102</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D1 live storage exceeds 5 GB</td>\n<td>8,489</td>\n<td>1,018</td>\n<td>157</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D1 physical writes exceed 50M</td>\n<td>14,815</td>\n<td>1,623</td>\n<td>246</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Workers requests exceed 10M</td>\n<td>27,592</td>\n<td>3,049</td>\n<td>462</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational log events exceed 20M</td>\n<td>28,550</td>\n<td>3,138</td>\n<td>476</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One D1 database exceeds 10 GB; needs sharding</td>\n<td>16,978</td>\n<td>2,036</td>\n<td>313</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A first crossing usually adds small metered charges, <strong>not an Enterprise-plan\nupgrade</strong>. In particular R2&#x27;s first extra billed GB is $0.015. Storage caps,\nidentity/role/organization-private-repo needs and required security features can\nforce architectural/plan changes independently of the bill. Required controls\ncannot be dropped to keep a price; unsupported strict/HSM requirements remain\nunavailable in the authorized software launch.</p>\n<h3 id=\"explicit-workload-formulas-and-cost-uncertainty\">Explicit workload formulas and cost uncertainty</h3>\n<p>For a cohort, P = payers × (included product profiles + one free profile),\nN = payers × (product successes + 30 free successes), A = twice payers. New\nprofile budget = ceil(10%P); migration assets = 10 × ceil(5%P); rewrap assets =\nceil(2%P). Q = ceil(1.08 × (N + 4 new profiles + migration assets + 2 rewrap assets)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2A). Software crypto work C = ceil(1.02 × (N + 2 new profiles + migration assets</li>\n<li>2 rewrap assets)) + 12 restore-drill work items. These include lifecycle/denied/\nretry overhead rather than pretending every request is a paid success.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CPU = 9Q + 5C ms. D1 reads = 8Q; physical writes = 8N + 2Q + 20 new profiles</p>\n<ul>\n<li>6 migration assets + 6 rewrap assets. Live DB bytes = 1,024N + 32,768P + 512Q;\nweb egress = 2,048Q bytes; DB egress = 8,192 × (N + migration assets + rewrap\nassets). Netlify Functions use 0.075 seconds × 1 GB/request. R2 holds two live DB\ncopies plus 12 months of 1 KiB logical/control audit events; backup writes =\n30 × (D1-sized shards + 1), recovery reads = 2 × shards. DB minimum shard count\nuses ceil(live GB/10). This is a sizing model, not a benchmark or actual retention\npolicy/qualified backup report. Real query/index/payload and identity traffic,\nforeground/background frequency, retries/abuse, archives, CPU and memory may differ.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is <strong>no invented monetary uncertainty/labor reserve in the hosting bill</strong>.\nUncertainty is exposed through workload assumptions, sleeping/continuously active\nDB branches, unknown Netlify storage and actual unused allowance/entitlement.\nRead back actual meters and replace assumptions before a spend commitment.\nThe observed price might be lower within existing free allowances or higher\nwith other apps, larger payloads, more DB units, functions memory or retention.\nThe existing model includes both 1-payer and 100-payer cases for every product\nworkload and both DB-active branches; it is not a customer tariff or margin model.</p>\n"},{"id":"part-6","title":"Free allowance and subscription opportunity","html":"<h2 id=\"free-allowance-and-subscription-opportunity\">Free allowance and subscription opportunity</h2>\n<p>Thirty combined successful hosted software crypto/age operations per verified\nfree owner is a demand assumption, not an enabled product grant. Metadata,\nidentity, encrypted storage, abuse admission and required audit still consume\nhosting resources. One free owner per payer is explicit; more free owners or\nabuse moves the breakpoints. Association creation, software wrap/unwrap, age\nfetch and retries retain separate typed usage/idempotency. Local/offline reuse\nadds no hosted use. No automatic charge, paid plan or quota downgrade follows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"illustrative-revenue-model\">Illustrative revenue model</h2>\n<p>Customer prices and margins are <strong>outside this hosting-only forecast</strong>. Previous\nage-only/native-key/labor-loaded price proposals are superseded for this question.\nNo payment fee, CRM or external KMS is hidden in the hosting subtotal. If later\nneeded, customer billing, optional paid managed SOFTWARE/HSM custody, payment\nfees or premium services require a separate explicit cost/entitlement decision;\nthey are not launch dependencies and no price is approved here.</p>\n<p>Recommendation: use the existing Cloudflare $5-plan branch first <strong>if</strong> its\nactual entitlements and a qualified existing/free customer issuer are verified.\nOtherwise evaluate existing Netlify included Identity without buying anything,\nwith DB active-hours and missing storage tariff made explicit. No current\naccount/project/issuer facts establish a qualified selected deployment. The\nselected launch custody tradeoff retains software KEK/runtime/shared-domain\nrisk; keep keys outside DB and enforce fundamental auth/isolation/audit/\nrecovery/tests. Funded managed/HSM migration stays versioned and staged, not a\nsilent assumption of present non-exportability. Synthetic/mock previews remain\nseparate from unapproved production UI.</p>\n<h2 id=\"runtime-sdk-and-agent-integration\">Runtime, SDK and agent integration</h2>\n<p>The service&#x27;s UI/API, CLI and optional MCP administration apply the same\nverified tenant, subject/client, scoped operation and time-window checks.\nOfficial <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-sdks&amp;part=part-1\">SDKs</a> explicitly select a local, workload-MCP or typed\nremote-management boundary. A local config write never becomes an implicit\nserver mutation. Agent/plugin administration is metadata-only and follows the\n<a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-agent-integrations&amp;part=part-1\">client integration contract</a>.</p>\n<p>The typed <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-management-and-discovery&amp;part=part-1\">discovery and management contract</a>\nprovides explicit remote-management API/SDK operations and a distinct optional\nmanagement MCP profile. <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-administration-security&amp;part=part-1\">Administration security</a>\nrequires signed change integrity, scoped authority, independent approval,\nanti-replay/rollback and durable audit. <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-security-profiles&amp;part=part-1\">Security profiles</a>\napply these requirements to every deployment and cannot be lowered by a client.</p>\n<p>Protected runtime policy supplies <a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-operational-configuration&amp;part=part-1\">observability and release awareness</a>.\nRequired administrative/release audit remains durable independently of\nbest-effort metrics, traces and diagnostics. Managed UI controls present these\nsame declarations and effective provenance; runtime telemetry does not depend\non a deployed UI. Collector credentials, bootstrap keys and retained age\nidentities never enter browser bundles or model-visible fields.</p>\n<p>Configuration/schema, service/API, SDK and artifact versions are explicit in\nmigration and compatibility records. Signed or authenticated update metadata\ncannot replace artifact provenance, per-environment promotion or local minimum\nversions. Incident recovery restores deny/revocation state before admitting\nrelease and does not promise to erase exported keys, data keys or plaintext.</p>\n<h2 id=\"operational-contract\">Operational contract</h2>\n<p>Operator setup, identity trust, entitlement checks, protected storage, key\nimport/generation, backup custody, grant migration, rotation, retention, and\nremoval follow <a href=\"/explore#doc=release&amp;part=part-1\">RELEASE.md</a> and the\n<a href=\"/explore#doc=docs-operators-recovery&amp;part=part-1\">recovery runbook</a>. Scope changes are reviewed; neither\nthis design nor a key-fetch request authorizes purchases, production changes,\nnew broad credentials, or service installation on an existing operational host.\nActual implementation, native/hardware/provider testing, capacity measurements,\nand release qualification are recorded separately in\n<a href=\"https://github.com/maldous/CircularQuay/blob/feature%2Finitial-implementation/docs/qualification.md\" title=\"Repository reference (may differ from this local snapshot)\">qualification.md</a>.</p>\n"}]};
